FINDINGS:
1. [MINOR] tests/stdlib_modules_test.rs:1322 — `path_normalize_keeps_a_relative_path_relative` hardcodes `"a/c"` as the expected answer to `path.normalize("a/./b/../c")`, but the implementation (`src/path.rs:373-383`, via `Path::components`/`to_string_lossy`) emits the platform separator. On Windows the same call answers `a\c`, so this new test fails on a platform the phase explicitly claims to support; the sibling tests at lines 1339-1343 and 1361-1364 correctly use `MAIN_SEPARATOR`, which shows the intent. Fix: build the expected text with `std::path::MAIN_SEPARATOR`. Could not run (read-only), but the output spelling is fixed by `Path::components` + `to_string_lossy`.
2. [MINOR] tests/stdlib_modules_test.rs:1349-1356 — `edge_normalize_a_parent_segment_above_a_root_stays_at_the_root` passes only the bare root (`"/"` or `"C:\"`) to `path.normalize`, with no `..` anywhere in the input. It therefore pins nothing about `..` climbing above a root, despite its name and the REPORT's test table claiming it does. The behavior is only covered by the unit test at `src/path.rs:664-668`, not by the both-engines integration path this test is meant to exercise. Fix: pass `format!("{root}..")` (and assert the result equals `root`).

